The conflict comes from two transforms on one element. The CSS `translate(-50%,-50%)` and GSAP's `x`/`y` both write to `transform`, so the tween's start value fights the CSS value. The lint rule `gsap_css_transform_conflict` catches this. Move the centering to a layout wrapper and leave the heading's transform entirely to GSAP.

```html
<div data-composition-id="intro" data-width="1920" data-height="1080" data-duration="4">
  <div class="center">
    <h1 id="intro-heading">Title</h1>
  </div>

  <style>
    .center {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
    }
    #intro-heading {
      margin: 0;
      font-size: 120px;
    }
  </style>

  <script>
    const tl = gsap.timeline({ paused: true });
    tl.fromTo(
      "#intro-heading",
      { x: -40, opacity: 0 },
      { x: 0, opacity: 1, duration: 1, ease: "power2.out" },
      0
    );
    window.__timelines["intro"] = tl;
  </script>
</div>
```

Why this works:

- **Centering** comes from flex in a full-bleed wrapper (`inset: 0`), so no `transform` is needed. The heading stays centered at every frame.
- **The animation** owns `transform` alone. `fromTo` sets the start state inside the tween instead of in CSS, which avoids the conflict.
- **Determinism:** the timeline is paused and registered once, under the root's `data-composition-id`. It has no clock reads, no randomness, and no network. Its length fits the root's `data-duration` of 4 seconds.

If the heading must keep a `translate`-based centering for some reason, use GSAP's `xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50` in the tween instead of the CSS transform. Note that this makes GSAP the only writer of `transform` on that element, so any other transform has to go on a parent.

I did not run `npx hyperframes check` on this. Run it after you apply the change to confirm the conflict is gone.

